 "content": {
  "subject": "Network Theory",
  "topic": "Series RLC Circuit Resonance",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Why does a sharper resonance curve actually mean a narrower bandwidth?",
  "question": "A series RLC circuit has a resonant frequency of 1000 radians per second and a quality factor of 10. What is its bandwidth?",
  "options": [
   "100 radians per second",
   "10000 radians per second",
   "10 radians per second",
   "50 radians per second"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 0,
  "answerLine": "The bandwidth of the circuit is 100 radians per second.",
  "explanation": [
   "Resonance in a series RLC network happens when inductive reactance equals capacitive reactance completely.",
   "At this resonant condition, the net reactive part vanishes and circuit impedance is purely resistive.",
   "Quality factor measures how selective the circuit is, defined as resonant frequency divided by bandwidth.",
   "Bandwidth represents the frequency spread between two half power points where power drops to half."
  ],
  "funFact": "Radio receivers use high quality factor series resonance to isolate one clean broadcast frequency.",
  "outro": "Remember that quality factor inversely scales bandwidth. Follow for more state engineering exam practice!",
